    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# ML and DL for Time Series\n",
        "\n",
        "## Theory\n",
        "\n",
        "## RNN\n",
        "\n",
        "*R*ecurrent *N*eural *N*etwork are useful with sequential data. It uses the concept of memory, meaning the previous information is used in the next steps. *Hidden state* makes possible the information sharing between layers.\n",
        "There is a problem: *vanishing gradient*, meaning the gradient tends to be smaller the longer the NN. This slows down the computation. LSTM and GRU help to solve this problem.\n",
        "*L*ong *S*hort *T*erm *M*emory, a type of RNN useful for time series.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Cell state** preserve the memory.\n",
        "- **Gates**, input door (**input**, **forget** and **output**) to manage the information flow and let the model save and drop informations.\n",
        "\n",
        "*G*ated *R*ecurrent *U*nit, a simplified LSTM which binds forget and input in a single gate to improve computational performance.\n",
        "\n",
        "*C*onvulutional *N*eural *N*etwork can be used for time series analysis. Good for local dependencies, bad on the long term one. Useful in combination with previous one.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Prophet\n",
        "\n",
        "Addictive model for time series with a strong seasonal, holiday and trend shift.\n",
        "\n",
        "$y(t) = g(t) + s(t) + h(t) + \\epsilon_t$\n",
        "\n",
        "- g(t) = trend.\n",
        "- s(t) = seasonality.\n",
        "- h(t) = holidays.\n",
        "- $\\epsilon_t$ = residual noise.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Pro\n",
        "\n",
        "- outlier and missing values handling.\n",
        "- robust.\n",
        "- quick training.\n",
        "- automatically find seasonality.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Cons\n",
        "\n",
        "- Less flexibility.\n",
        "- Addictive model does not work well with multiplicative data, for example for variance.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Accuracy\n",
        "\n",
        "### Metrics \n",
        "\n",
        "*M*ean *A*bsolute *E*rror: $MAE = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} |Y_i - \\hat{Y_i}|$\n",
        "  \n",
        "- avg absolute error -> precision.\n",
        "- robust to outlier.\n",
        "\n",
        "*R*oot *MSE*: $RMSE = \\sqrt{ \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_i - \\hat{Y_i})^2 }$\n",
        "\n",
        "  - better penality for big errors. \n",
        "\n",
        "*M*ean *A*bsolute *P*ercentage *E*rror: $MAPE = \\frac{100}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\left| \\frac{Y_i - \\hat{Y_i}}{Y_i} \\right|$\n",
        "\n",
        "  - Mean of the percentage errors.\n",
        "  - It does not depends on the data scale.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Plots\n",
        "\n",
        "Using plots, *forecast vs actuals*, *residual analysis*.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Model comparison\n",
        "\n",
        "- Benchmarking with naive models.\n",
        "- Cross validation.\n",
        "\n",
        "  - avoid data leakage and time dependencies.\n",
        "  - makes the model robust to new data.\n",
        "\n",
        "  - techniques:\n",
        "    \n",
        "    - Fixed or Rolling forecast origin, which fit on a moving window and test it on the next value.\n",
        "    - Time series split, keeps the order of the observations.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Practice"
      ],
      "id": "fdbe63ac"
    },
